# Particles
@scenario tutorial_lesson_23
@include /definitions/characters.vns
@label start
[mode dialogue standard]
[set ui.dialogueUi project]
[bg room]
[char ginger global on]
[set ui.dialogueFadeMs 160]
[stage clear]
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/23_particles_and_visualizers.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt dust]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: This example starts the dust emitter for three seconds. Observe the particles against the white background and check how easily you can read the dialogue.
[if !tutorial_still]
# region dust
[particles dust intensity=0.24 opacity=0.52 speed=0.30 duration=3000]
# endregion
ginger: The intensity is {b}0.24{/b}. Speed and opacity are set separately. Advance to stop the emitter before the next comparison.
[else]
ginger: Reduced motion is enabled. The example skips the emitter and continues to the same exercise.
[endif]
# region stop
[particles stop]
# endregion
ginger: Change intensity from {color=#FFD7A3}0.24{/color} to 0.48. Leave speed and opacity alone. Compare the density and the readability of the text.
[set tutorial_excerpt stop]
ginger: Use [particles stop] at the end of an effect if the following section should start with a clear background.
ginger: Duration sets an upper time for this effect. An explicit stop can end it earlier when the player advances. Record whether you waited on the line before comparing two runs.
ginger: Intensity, opacity and speed answer different questions. How full does the effect look, how visible is each particle, and how quickly does it travel? Change one field per comparison.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Compare particle settings independently -> practice
> Review question -> check
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label check
ginger: Which comparison shows whether the effect affects readability?
> Read the same line with the effect on and off. -> correct
> Check the particle count alone. -> retry
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label retry
[show ginger guide confused]
ginger: The particle count alone doesn't tell you how clearly the words stand out. Compare the same text in both conditions.
[jump check]
@label correct
[show ginger guide normal]
ginger: Read the same line with the effect enabled and disabled, using the same window size.
> Back to topics -> return_hub
> Replay example -> start
@label return_hub
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
[if !tutorial_done_23]
[flag tutorial_done_23]
[inc tutorial_count]
[endif]
[stage clear]
[goto TutorialHub:topics_page_3]
[end]

@label practice
[show ginger beside_code thoughtful]
[set tutorial_source scripts/tutorial/23_particles_and_visualizers.vns]
[set tutorial_excerpt dust]
[set ui.dialogueFacet tutorial_code]
ginger: Run the original intensity, 0.24, against the white background. Pick one word in the dialogue and watch the space around it for a fixed interval.
ginger: Double intensity to 0.48 and keep speed, opacity and duration fixed. Compare the density across several frames. A random emitter will not put the same particle at the same point each time.
ginger: Restore intensity and lower only opacity. The particles should become less conspicuous even if the emitter is still producing them. Then restore opacity and change speed to compare motion.
ginger: Test a rapid advance and a slow read. On the rapid route the stop instruction can cut the effect short. On the slow route the configured duration can expire first.
@label practice_question
ginger: The effect ends sooner only when the player advances quickly. Which instruction explains it?
> The explicit stop reached after the dialogue. -> practice_result
> The unchanged opacity value. -> practice_hint
> Back to topics -> return_hub
@label practice_hint
ginger: Opacity affects visibility. Follow the script beyond the line that waits for player input and find the instruction that ends the emitter.
[jump practice_question]
@label practice_result
ginger: The advance changes when execution reaches stop. Measure the interval from effect start to that instruction on both routes.
[clear ui.dialogueFacet]
> Review question -> check
> Try the exercise again -> practice
> Back to topics -> return_hub
